    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Describe the bug\n\nI hit this in a production system on Windows: a string constant defined in a .pyx file came out truncated at 2**16 characters after building the extension, with a completely clean build log. It affects both `\"...\"` and `b\"...\"` literals. The same code works fine on Linux (gcc), which is why it took me a while to track down.\n\nAfter digging in, the root cause turned out to be MSVC, not the generated code — cl.exe silently emits only `len mod 65536` bytes of a string literal's initializer data when the (concatenated) literal is longer than 65535 bytes. No error, no warning, even at /W3. But since Cython already splits long literals into 2000-char pieces to work around another MSVC limit (`StringEncoding.split_string_literal`), I think Cython is the right place to also work around this one — or at least fail loudly instead of shipping corrupted constants.\n\nI searched the tracker for similar issues; the closest are #4924 (link.exe hangs with many constants) and #5290 (big integer literals fail on MSVC), but neither covers this. I also checked current master:\n`split_string_literal` still only does the 2000-char piece splitting and nothing limits the total literal size, so master generates the same affected code as the 3.0.10 I tested with.\n\n### Code to reproduce the behaviour:\n\n```bat\npython -c \"a='A'*(2**16+3); print(f'a=\\\"{a}\\\"\\nprint(a)')\" > main.pyx\npython setup.py build_ext --inplace\npython -c \"import main\"\n```\n\nwith this setup.py:\n\n```python\nfrom setuptools import setup, Extension\nfrom Cython.Build import cythonize\n\next = Extension(name=\"main\", sources=[\"main.pyx\"], language=\"c++\")\nsetup(ext_modules=cythonize(ext, compiler_directives={'language_level': 3}))\n```\n\nActual output:\n\n```\nC:\\Users\\win10\\Desktop\\cythonbugtest>python -c \"import main\"\nAAAA\n```\n\nExactly 4 characters, because 65539 chars + 1 NUL terminator = 65540 bytes and 65540 mod 65536 = 4. The build log contains no warning or error at all.\n\n### Expected behaviour\n\n`print(a)` prints all 65539 characters (as it does on Linux with gcc, and as plain CPython does when the same file is executed as a .py module). If the target compiler genuinely can't represent the constant, I'd expect the build to fail with an error rather than produce a silently corrupted extension.\n\n### OS\n\nWindows 10 x64\n\n### Python version\n\n3.11.9 (MSC v.1938, 64 bit)\n\n### Cython version\n\n3.0.10\n\n### Additional context\n\n_No response_",
